[{"data":1,"prerenderedAt":161},["ShallowReactive",2],{"module-freshplayerplugin":3},{"generatedAt":4,"module":5,"related":95,"aliases":160},"2026-11-22T11:41:54.252Z",{"name":6,"version":7,"repo":8,"path":9,"section":10,"short":11,"description":12,"website":13,"source":14,"entered":15,"updated":16,"depends":17,"dependents":70,"bringsAlong":71,"questions":72,"lastChange":73,"history":78},"freshplayerplugin","v0.3.11","other","video\u002Ffreshplayerplugin","video","PPAPI-host NPAPI-plugin adapter","As you know, Adobe have suspended further development of Flash player plugin for GNU\u002FLinux.\nLatest available as an NPAPI plugin version 11.2 will get security updates for five years\n(since its release on May 4th, 2012), but further development have been ceased. Fortunately\nor not, newer versions are still available for Linux as a part of Chrome browser, where Flash\ncomes bundled in a form of PPAPI plugin. PPAPI or Pepper Plugin API is an interface promoted\nby Chromium\u002FChrome team for browser plugins. It's NPAPI-inspired yet significantly different\nAPI which have every concievable function plugin may want.\n\nFor various reasons Firefox developers are not interested now in implementing PPAPI in Firefox.\nHowever that does not mean it cannot be done.\n\nThe main goal of this project is to get PPAPI (Pepper) Flash player working in Firefox.\n\nDo note freshplayerplugin at this time does not implement any sandboxing.","https:\u002F\u002Fgithub.com\u002Fi-rinat\u002Ffreshplayerplugin","https:\u002F\u002Fgithub.com\u002Fi-rinat\u002Ffreshplayerplugin\u002Farchive\u002Fv0.3.11.tar.gz","2014-08-02","2024-03-02",[18,20,22,24,26,28,30,32,34,36,38,40,42,44,46,48,53,57,61,65],{"module":19},"ragel",{"module":21},"alsa-utils",{"module":23},"v4l-utils",{"module":25},"glib-2",{"module":27},"mesa-lib",{"module":29},"xorgproto",{"module":31},"cmake",{"module":33},"libglvnd",{"module":35},"uriparser",{"module":37},"libconfig",{"module":39},"libevent",{"module":41},"cairo",{"m